1
00:00:02,400 --> 00:00:05,324
Figure one. First run.

2
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:20,596
One line installs Keelson into a single folder on your machine. The command line and your workflows live there now, and your database and ribs will too. No hosted service, and no Keelson account.

3
00:00:21,766 --> 00:00:30,548
So, is it healthy? Keelson doctor checks the toolchain, the store, your credentials, and your workflows, in one pass.

4
00:00:30,776 --> 00:00:42,084
Two warnings, and both are expected. There's no server yet, and no database. Each warning names its own fix, and here it's the same one: start the server.

5
00:00:43,184 --> 00:00:54,572
That one command is the heart of it. It runs a single local server that owns the store, the agents, the browser app, and an endpoint other agents can call, all on one port.

6
00:00:56,392 --> 00:00:58,700
Start the server, then ask again.

7
00:01:01,012 --> 00:01:03,962
Zero warnings. Zero failures.

8
00:01:05,012 --> 00:01:10,690
Copilot is the default agent, so once you're signed in to it, send one real message.

9
00:01:13,612 --> 00:01:23,992
That question went from the command line to the server, out to Copilot, and back. And the turn was saved locally, so the browser already shows the same conversation.

10
00:01:24,692 --> 00:01:29,968
That's a first run. One folder, one server, and a doctor that tells you what to do next.

11
00:01:32,668 --> 00:01:36,216
Keelson. Lay the keel, raise the ribs.

